  Here is a breakdown of where values in the Nutanix
  driver come from, what is computed by the driver, and
   what is deliberately left empty.

  Three sources of truth

  Values in nutanix.py fall into three buckets:

  ┌────────┬───────────────────────┬──────────────────┐
  │ Source │ Examples              │ Documented?      │
  ├────────┼───────────────────────┼──────────────────┤
  │ Nutani │ memorySizeBytes,      │ Yes — Nutanix v4 │
  │ x      │ powerState,           │ OpenAPI / Prism  │
  │ v4 API │ nics[].networkInfo,   │ Central REST     │
  │        │ task entitiesAffected │                  │
  ├────────┼───────────────────────┼──────────────────┤
  │ Libclo │ SYNTHETIC_SIZES,      │ Driver design    │
  │ ud     │ MiB↔bytes, volume     │ (see conceptual  │
  │ adapte │ state from            │ guide)           │
  │ r      │ attachments           │                  │
  │ logic  │                       │                  │
  ├────────┼───────────────────────┼──────────────────┤
  │ Guest  │ SSH public key in     │ cloud-init spec; │
  │ OS /   │ #cloud-config         │ Nutanix passes   │
  │ cloud- │                       │ it via           │
  │ init   │                       │ guestCustomizati │
  │        │                       │ on               │
  └────────┴───────────────────────┴──────────────────┘

  ────────────────────────────────────────

  1. Derived values — Nutanix API shape vs Libcloud 
  shape

  Unit conversions (Libcloud ↔ Nutanix)

  Nutanix v4 uses bytes for memory and disk; Libcloud
  NodeSize.ram uses MiB and volumes use GiB. The driver
   converts at the boundary:

   libcloud/common/nutanix.py lines 332-337

  def mib_to_bytes(mib):
      return mib * 1024 * 1024
  def bytes_to_mib(value):
      return int(value / (1024 * 1024))

   libcloud/common/nutanix.py lines 371-371

          "memorySizeBytes": mib_to_bytes(memory_mib),

  That is not Nutanix-specific logic — it is standard
  binary unit conversion so Libcloud callers can keep
  using MiB/GiB while the API payload matches the
  schema (memorySizeBytes, diskSizeBytes). Nutanix
  documents those field names in the v4 OpenAPI
  (referenced in your conceptual guide as
  stoplight_mock/spec/openapi.json).

  Synthetic sizes (not from Nutanix at all)

   libcloud/common/nutanix.py lines 76-79

  SYNTHETIC_SIZES = {
      "small": {"vcpus": 1, "cores_per_vcpu": 1, 
  "memory_mib": 2048, "disk_mib": 20480},
      ...
  }

  Unlike AWS instance types or DigitalOcean droplet
  sizes, Nutanix has no fixed “flavor catalog” in this 
  driver. list_sizes() returns four hard-coded presets
  purely so Libcloud’s portable create_node(name, size,
   image, …) API has something to pass. Nutanix does
  not expose an equivalent list endpoint; you specify
  numSockets, numCoresPerSocket, and memorySizeBytes
  directly on VM create. This is documented in
  NUTANIX_LIBCLOUD_CONCEPTUAL_GUIDE.md §6.

  State mapping (Nutanix enum → Libcloud enum)

   libcloud/compute/drivers/nutanix.py lines 89-94

      NODE_STATE_MAP = {
          "ON": NodeState.RUNNING,
          "POWERED_ON": NodeState.RUNNING,
          "OFF": NodeState.STOPPED,
          ...
      }

  powerState comes from the Nutanix VM API; the driver
  maps it to portable NodeState. Same pattern for
  volume/snapshot status fields.

  Volume size and state (derived from related 
  resources)

   libcloud/compute/drivers/nutanix.py lines 1199-1207

          total_bytes = 0
          for disk in disks:
              total_bytes += disk.get("diskSizeBytes") 
  or 0
          ...
          state = (
              StorageVolumeState.INUSE
              if attached_vm_ext_ids
              else StorageVolumeState.AVAILABLE
          )

  Volume group GET may not give a single “size” field
  the way Libcloud expects, so size is summed from 
  child disks. State is inferred from VM attachments
  when the API status is absent or incomplete —
  Libcloud convention, not a Nutanix catalog item.

  Task completion → entity ID (Nutanix Prism, 
  documented pattern)

  Async creates return a task; the created
  VM/image/snapshot ID is read from entitiesAffected or
   completionDetails:

   libcloud/common/nutanix.py lines 597-601

  def extract_entity_ext_id_from_task(task):
      affected = task.get("entitiesAffected") or 
  task.get("entities_affected") or []
      ...
  def extract_task_completion_detail(task, key):
      details = task.get("completionDetails") or 
  task.get("completion_details") or []

  This matches the Nutanix v4 async task model
  described in your conceptual guide (§3.4).

  SSH keys via cloud-init (OS feature, Nutanix as 
  carrier)

   libcloud/compute/drivers/nutanix.py lines 286-290

          if auth is not None and hasattr(auth, 
  "pubkey"):
              guest_customization = guest_customization
   or {}
              
  guest_customization.setdefault("cloudInit", 
  {})["userData"] = (
                  
  "#cloud-config\nssh_authorized_keys:\n  - %s\n" % 
  auth.pubkey
              )

  The format is cloud-init (#cloud-config,
  ssh_authorized_keys) — a Linux/guest-OS convention.
  Nutanix’s role is to accept it in
  guestCustomization.cloudInit.userData on VM create
  (documented in Nutanix guest customization /
  cloud-init integration). Nutanix does not store or
  return SSH keys as a first-class API resource; there
  is no Nutanix “key pair” API in this driver.

  ────────────────────────────────────────

  2. Why IP addresses are often empty — nothing 
  substituted

  This is the most important “not substituted” case.

  On create: subnet reference only, no static IP in 
  payload

   libcloud/common/nutanix.py lines 405-412

      elif subnet_ext_id:
          payload["nics"] = [
              {
                  "networkInfo": {
                      "subnet": {"extId": 
  subnet_ext_id},
                  }
              }
          ]

  You attach a subnet (extId), not an IP address.
  Nutanix networking assigns addresses via IPAM/DHCP or
   the guest configures them after boot. The v4 VM
  create schema does not require (and often does not
  accept) a static IP in this minimal NIC shape unless
  you build a richer ex_nics payload yourself.

  On read: IPs only if Nutanix populated 
  nics[].networkInfo

   libcloud/common/nutanix.py lines 309-316

  def extract_ips_from_nics(nics):
      public_ips = []
      private_ips = []
      ...
                  if value:
                      private_ips.append(value)
      return public_ips, private_ips

  IPs are read back from whatever Nutanix stored under
  networkInfo.ipv4.ipAddresses / ipv6. That structure
  is Nutanix v4 API schema. If the API returns no
  addresses (VM off, guest not booted, DHCP not done,
  Nutanix hasn’t learned the IP yet), nothing is 
  substituted — you get empty lists.

  Your fixtures illustrate this:

  • web-01 has "ipAddresses": [{"value": 
    "10.0.0.10"}] → appears in private_ips
  • db-01 has "nics": [] → no IPs at all

  public_ips is always empty

   libcloud/common/nutanix.py lines 309-311

      public_ips = []
      ...
      return public_ips, private_ips

  Every address goes into private_ips. Nutanix v4 NIC
  JSON does not label addresses as “public” vs
  “private” the way AWS or Vultr do. Floating/external
  IPs live under a separate floating-ips API
  (ex_list_load_balancers), and that path is not wired 
  into Node.public_ips. So public_ips stays [] even
  when private IPs exist — a Libcloud mapping choice,
  not something Nutanix documents as a VM NIC field.

  Immediately after create_node: placeholder with empty
   IPs

   libcloud/compute/drivers/nutanix.py lines 328-334

          return Node(
              id=task_ext_id or name,
              ...
              public_ips=[],
              private_ips=[],
              ...
          )

  If task wait fails or entity ID isn’t resolved, the
  driver returns a pending Node with no IPs. Even when
  wait succeeds and ex_get_node runs, IPs still depend
  on what Nutanix has in the GET response at that
  moment — often still empty right after create.

  ────────────────────────────────────────

  3. Other “optional / not filled in” patterns

  ┌────────────┬───────────────┬──────────────────────┐
  │ Field      │ Behavior      │ Why                  │
  ├────────────┼───────────────┼──────────────────────┤
  │ NodeLocati │ Always None   │ Clusters have no     │
  │ on.country │               │ country in Nutanix   │
  │            │               │ API                  │
  ├────────────┼───────────────┼──────────────────────┤
  │ NodeSize.b │ None / 0.0    │ Not applicable       │
  │ andwidth   │               │ on-prem              │
  │ / price    │               │                      │
  ├────────────┼───────────────┼──────────────────────┤
  │ build_subn │ Only sent if  │ Subnet CIDR is       │
  │ et_create_ │ ip_address    │ optional in API;     │
  │ payload    │ and           │ omitted if caller    │
  │ IP fields  │ prefix_length │ doesn’t pass them    │
  │            │ provided      │                      │
  ├────────────┼───────────────┼──────────────────────┤
  │ ex_create_ │ Optional      │ Nutanix can          │
  │ load_balan │               │ auto-assign floating │
  │ cer        │               │ IP if omitted        │
  │ external_i │               │                      │
  │ p          │               │                      │
  ├────────────┼───────────────┼──────────────────────┤
  │ Default    │ Used when     │ Driver default, not  │
  │ disk size  │ image set but │ Nutanix API default  │
  │ 20480 MiB  │ no            │                      │
  │            │ disk_size_mib │                      │
  └────────────┴───────────────┴──────────────────────┘

  ────────────────────────────────────────

  Summary diagram

  mermaid flowchart

  flowchart LR
    subgraph nutanix_api [Nutanix v4 API]
      VM[VM JSON]
      Task[Prism Task]
      NIC[nics.networkInfo]
    end
    subgraph driver [Libcloud driver]
      Convert[MiB/bytes, state maps]
      Synthetic[SYNTHETIC_SIZES]
      IPs[extract_ips_from_nics]
    end
    subgraph guest [Guest OS]
      CI[cloud-init SSH keys]
    end
    VM --> Convert
    VM --> IPs
    Task --> Convert
    Synthetic --> Convert
    CI -->|guestCustomization| VM
    NIC -->|"only if API populated"| IPs
    IPs -->|"public_ips: never filled"| Empty[[]]

  ────────────────────────────────────────

